Syntax: leap <direction>

Through rigorous training, monks learn to channel their inner energy into
powerful leaps that carry them through the air to adjacent rooms. This
technique, inspired by the effortless grace of cats, allows rapid movement
that bypasses many obstacles.

When executing a catleap, the monk springs into the air and soars in the
specified direction. The leap requires solid ground to push off from and
cannot be performed while in combat or when exhausted. Each leap consumes
movement points.

Success depends on the monk's mastery of the technique. A failed leap
results in a short, embarrassing hop that leaves the monk sitting on
the ground rather than arriving at their destination. Masters of catleap
rarely fail, moving with fluid grace that seems almost supernatural.

The catleap technique is related to catfall, which protects monks from
falling damage. Together, these skills make monks remarkably mobile in
vertical environments.

See Also: CATFALL, SPRINGLEAP
